  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 316 AM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES WV imagery place the UP in the middle of a closed \nlow over the Lower Great Lakes and a shortwave over northwestern \nOntario. Beneath these features is high pressure extending from \nQuebec across Upper MI and down to the Mid-Mississippi River Valley, \nyielding calm conditions. Favorable radiative cooling in the west \nhas brought temps down into the 20s. Stubborn low stratus still \nstreaming over the east has helped insulate, keeping temps in the \n30s. The rest of today remains dry as high pressure dissipates and \nshifts southeast. The shortwave to the north pivots northeast away \nfrom the region, but is quickly replaced with a deeper more vigorous \ntrough for tonight into Sunday. Temps warm into the mid 40s to low \n50s today with plenty of sunshine.\n\nWarmer tonight in the 30s to low 40s as southwest WAA picks up. A \nclipper system tracking over far northern Ontario on Sunday sends a \ncold front across the CWA Sunday morning. Breezy west winds are \nexpected in the wake of that front gusting 20-30 mph courtesy of CAA \nand pressure rises. Higher gusts are expected in the Keweenaw and \nnear Lake Superior up to 35-40 mph. Temps once again peak in the 40s \nto low 50s early in the afternoon. This system also manages to clip \nthe northeastern half of the CWA with lake effect/upslope rain \nshowers (mainly 20-50% chance). \n\nThe cycle repeats itself early next week. Ridging in the wake of \nthis clipper results in another dry period with lighter winds Monday \nthrough Tuesday morning. The next shortwave in the clipper train \nlooks to arrive Tuesday afternoon, though once again the low looks \ndisplaced too far north and too weak to drive synoptic precipitation \nfor the UP. Colder air in the wake of the clipper will bring lake \neffect precipitation chances (20-40%) that persist through late week \nas winds turn northerly. Guidance has trended back on gust \npotential, not expecting widespread gusts like Sunday's forecast. \nTemps return near normal with highs in the mid 30s to low 40s and \nlows in the 20s to low 30s.",
